1
00:00:06,166 --> 00:00:07,666
[whirring, beeping]

2
00:00:09,299 --> 00:00:10,799
[smooth instrumental music playing]

3
00:00:17,733 --> 00:00:19,433
[whirring, beeping]

4
00:00:21,366 --> 00:00:25,566
[engine starts, revs]

5
00:00:32,299 --> 00:00:36,666
[Jerry] This is a 1964 Aston Martin DB5
in silver birch

6
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:41,133
with an all-aluminum
280-horsepower straight-six.

7
00:00:41,199 --> 00:00:44,000
Yes, yes. Oh, yes.

8
00:00:44,066 --> 00:00:45,400
This is the one.

9
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,000
No other car could possibly have been
exactly what James Bond needed

10
00:00:52,099 --> 00:00:53,800
when it was time to save the world.

11
00:00:56,733 --> 00:00:59,433
It's dapper, it's dashing,
it's distinguished,

12
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:00,699
it's deadly.

13
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:07,666
In comedy, we are also
out to save the world.

14
00:01:07,733 --> 00:01:10,966
And sometimes,
a very special agent is required.

15
00:01:11,366 --> 00:01:15,233
Someone who can take written material
and turn it into a very deadly weapon.

16
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:18,800
Very few people
possess these special skills.

17
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:22,133
My guest today
is one of those very special agents,

18
00:01:22,515 --> 00:01:24,517
Miss Julia Louis-Dreyfus.

19
00:01:25,133 --> 00:01:30,033
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

20
00:01:30,899 --> 00:01:32,899
[musical flourish]

21
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:37,033
[laughing]

22
00:01:38,966 --> 00:01:41,633
[Jerry] Remember this? You remember this?

23
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:43,733
-Remember this?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

24
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:44,733
[Jerry laughing]

25
00:01:44,833 --> 00:01:46,666
-Okay, so...
-Oh, boy.

26
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:48,908
-I'm nervous.
-Don't give me the work face.

27
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:50,733
-Don't give me the work face.
-Okay.

28
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:53,000
[smooth instrumental music playing]

29
00:01:53,899 --> 00:01:57,000
[exclaiming excitedly] Oh! Oh-ho-ho!

30
00:01:57,217 --> 00:02:01,087
[Julia] Oh, my God.
It's unbelievable-looking.

31
00:02:01,166 --> 00:02:02,933
-It's chic.
-[Jerry] Yeah, well, that's you.

32
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,899
-Thanks, that's nice of you.
-You're welcome.

33
00:02:04,991 --> 00:02:08,161
-[Julia] Look at the wheels.
-[Jerry] I know. Borrani wires.

34
00:02:08,266 --> 00:02:10,633
-That's what I was gonna say.
-Yeah. [chuckles]

35
00:02:10,733 --> 00:02:15,100
It's English, high-end,
'cause you are the James Bond of comedy.

36
00:02:15,233 --> 00:02:18,633
Whatever the mission,
whatever the situation,

37
00:02:18,766 --> 00:02:20,833
you come in and kill it.

38
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:22,466
-That is, first of all...
-Right?

39
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:24,700
...the nicest thing
anyone has ever said to me.

40
00:02:24,777 --> 00:02:28,381
I have never seen anything
quite like this. Isn't it unbelievable?

41
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:31,700
Oh, my God! Look at this thing.

42
00:02:31,799 --> 00:02:33,866
[man, honks horn]
Hey, Jerry, I'll trade ya.

43
00:02:33,966 --> 00:02:35,200
[Julia laughing]

44
00:02:36,066 --> 00:02:37,799
I'm so tired of that line.

45
00:02:37,866 --> 00:02:40,033
-So tired of it.
-You poor man.

46
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:40,933
[chuckling]

47
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:43,366
You know what else
I can't take another second of?

48
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:46,033
One, two...

49
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:47,700
Just take the picture.

50
00:02:47,766 --> 00:02:50,500
Nothing happens on three, okay?

51
00:02:50,933 --> 00:02:53,033
I'm not gonna get happy on three.

52
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:57,000
[smooth instrumental music playing]

53
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:03,933
[Julia] I'm not a car person,
but I must say

54
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,600
that it's so lovely, this car.

55
00:03:09,366 --> 00:03:11,666
How about this?
They could let this go for a song.

56
00:03:11,724 --> 00:03:13,092
[Julia] Let me tell you something.

57
00:03:13,159 --> 00:03:16,196
This house is like the worst of a woman
who lives in Los Angeles,

58
00:03:16,266 --> 00:03:20,666
constantly doing surgery,
and is unrecognizable and hideous.

59
00:03:20,733 --> 00:03:23,133
One of those horrible housewives
or something.

60
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:26,899
That would've been a much better title
than The Real Housewives.

61
00:03:26,966 --> 00:03:28,733
Those Horrible Housewives.

62
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:36,299
-[horn honks]
-[Julia] Oh. Okay.

63
00:03:36,366 --> 00:03:37,566
-What's happening?
-[chuckling]

64
00:03:37,633 --> 00:03:40,633
I'm so sorry.
We're doing the Seinfeld reunion.

65
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:42,299
And you're not in it.

66
00:03:42,366 --> 00:03:44,500
[Julia laughs] Bye.

67
00:03:45,566 --> 00:03:47,200
See, because you're so charming,

68
00:03:47,266 --> 00:03:49,266
you got away with insulting her,

69
00:03:49,533 --> 00:03:50,933
-profoundly.
-Thank you.

70
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,200
Do you think I'm nice?

71
00:03:52,299 --> 00:03:53,799
I think you can be nice.

72
00:03:54,766 --> 00:03:56,666
Anyone can be nice.

73
00:03:56,766 --> 00:03:58,133
Hitler can be nice.

74
00:03:58,233 --> 00:03:59,799
Well, you're nicer than Hitler.

75
00:04:02,299 --> 00:04:05,066
-Oh, hey! How's it going?
-[woman] Hey!

76
00:04:05,133 --> 00:04:07,566
-Oh, my God!
-[Julia] How hilarious.

77
00:04:07,633 --> 00:04:09,399
You don't bring any attention to yourself.

78
00:04:09,466 --> 00:04:11,000
-[Jerry laughing]
-[Julia] Okay.

79
00:04:11,066 --> 00:04:12,166
That was Hilary Swank...

80
00:04:12,233 --> 00:04:14,733
-I know!
-...and I guess we must live in Hollywood.

81
00:04:14,799 --> 00:04:16,333
[upbeat music playing]

82
00:04:17,233 --> 00:04:18,633
[Julia] Do you remember...

83
00:04:18,691 --> 00:04:22,061
I love anything you say that starts with,
"Do you remember?"

84
00:04:22,133 --> 00:04:23,633
[laughing]

85
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:28,166
Do you remember when I was about
five months pregnant with my youngest,

86
00:04:28,234 --> 00:04:30,937
and big as a house, and you came up--

87
00:04:31,004 --> 00:04:32,839
-Okay. Here we go.
-I know where this is going.

88
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:35,266
And you came up to me, and you said,

89
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:39,800
"Hey, we had a great idea.
How about if we write it in this season

90
00:04:39,866 --> 00:04:41,700
that Elaine just gets fat?"

91
00:04:42,766 --> 00:04:46,099
Don't get in a car accident
from that reminiscence. Hi.

92
00:04:46,533 --> 00:04:48,599
And so, what did I do?

93
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:51,500
Burst into tears. Automatic.

94
00:04:51,599 --> 00:04:53,366
It was like a death sentence.

95
00:04:53,433 --> 00:04:55,366
So, there are two things
I have to say about that.

96
00:04:55,433 --> 00:04:58,599
One is, you have
no interpersonal communication skills.

97
00:04:58,698 --> 00:05:00,466
-That's not true.
-The second thing is,

98
00:05:00,533 --> 00:05:02,168
it was a great idea
and we should've done it.

99
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:03,099
[gasps]

100
00:05:03,166 --> 00:05:06,333
-I can't believe you're saying this.
-It would've been a great storyline.

101
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:07,866
Yeah, it would've been pretty funny.

102
00:05:07,933 --> 00:05:12,633
And it really shows
what a great comic actress you are,

103
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:15,400
that you thought about it,
and eventually got to,

104
00:05:15,966 --> 00:05:17,599
"That was the funny direction to go."

105
00:05:17,666 --> 00:05:20,366
Yeah. I actually... I regret it.

106
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,200
[upbeat music playing]

107
00:05:26,125 --> 00:05:28,161
[inaudible]

108
00:05:31,533 --> 00:05:34,766
The other thing that I think about

109
00:05:34,833 --> 00:05:36,533
is what happened to my horse.

110
00:05:37,533 --> 00:05:38,933
Hey, how did my... [giggles]

111
00:05:41,466 --> 00:05:43,133
[Julia] And you know who that is, right?

112
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:44,500
It's Tracy Letts,

113
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:47,766
who went on to win the Pulitzer Prize.

114
00:05:47,833 --> 00:05:49,900
He wrote August: Osage County.

115
00:05:50,966 --> 00:05:53,200
Yes. That is exactly right.

116
00:05:53,266 --> 00:05:55,966
I said, "Hey, how'd my horse do?"
[chuckles] And he said...

117
00:05:56,466 --> 00:05:57,633
He had to be shot.

118
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:00,766
Which, to this day, is so deeply funny.

119
00:06:00,833 --> 00:06:02,566
Why do you think it's so funny?

120
00:06:02,633 --> 00:06:07,233
Because it's so teed up for hope,
and that's why it's funny.

121
00:06:07,966 --> 00:06:10,433
-"Line!" That's another thing I remember.
-[Jerry laughing]

122
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:11,466
[Julia laughing]

123
00:06:11,533 --> 00:06:12,900
-"Line!"
-[laughing]

124
00:06:14,099 --> 00:06:16,666
Like it's the other person's fault...

125
00:06:16,766 --> 00:06:18,400
-Yeah.
-...that I forgot my line.

126
00:06:18,466 --> 00:06:19,433
[both laughing]

127
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,200
See, we had fun on that show.

128
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,333
[upbeat jazz music playing]

129
00:06:35,533 --> 00:06:37,500
-I'm so happy to see you.
-I'm so happy to see you.

130
00:06:37,566 --> 00:06:39,733
-Remember when we were doing--
-The parking garage?

131
00:06:42,066 --> 00:06:43,900
How did you know I was gonna say that?

132
00:06:43,966 --> 00:06:45,700
Because I knew you were gonna say it.

133
00:06:50,133 --> 00:06:51,866
Did you ever want to work at a coffee bar?

134
00:06:52,078 --> 00:06:53,813
[Julia]
No, but I was an ice cream scooper.

135
00:06:54,266 --> 00:06:55,799
-But I...
-Oh, yeah. [laughs]

136
00:06:55,966 --> 00:06:57,400
Yeah, that lasted a week.

137
00:06:57,466 --> 00:07:00,533
What happened was,
whoever was working the cash register

138
00:07:00,599 --> 00:07:01,866
had to leave or something,

139
00:07:02,088 --> 00:07:04,056
so they said, "Will you
go work the cash register?"

140
00:07:04,133 --> 00:07:06,366
And I said, "Okay."
I don't know how to work the register.

141
00:07:06,433 --> 00:07:09,833
The cash register ran out of tape.

142
00:07:09,933 --> 00:07:12,900
And the drawer was open,
so I went to go find somebody to help me,

143
00:07:12,965 --> 00:07:15,668
and I sort of walked away
with the register open.

144
00:07:15,766 --> 00:07:20,000
The manager, who I think was 18,
but it seemed like he was 40,

145
00:07:21,099 --> 00:07:22,033
came out, and he says,

146
00:07:22,133 --> 00:07:25,566
"Do you have any idea
what you just did wrong, young lady?"

147
00:07:25,633 --> 00:07:28,033
Which I just... [mimicking sobbing]

148
00:07:28,166 --> 00:07:29,533
Started crying so hard.

149
00:07:29,966 --> 00:07:31,066
[Jerry] You cry a lot.

150
00:07:31,299 --> 00:07:32,833
[Julia] Look. Isn't that gorgeous?

151
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:35,666
[Jerry] "I lay this [bleep] before you."
Remember that one?

152
00:07:35,721 --> 00:07:38,324
Jason would finish his speech,
and he would do this.

153
00:07:38,391 --> 00:07:39,592
I go, "What does that mean?"

154
00:07:39,666 --> 00:07:43,166
-He goes, "I lay this [bleep] before you."
-Yeah.

155
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,299
Look at this.

156
00:07:48,366 --> 00:07:50,233
[softly] Wow, Julia, this is really good.

157
00:07:50,299 --> 00:07:52,966
How can you tell it's so good,
just 'cause it feels and looks good?

158
00:07:53,033 --> 00:07:55,433
Yes. Feels good, looks good, is good.

159
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:57,233
[laughing]

160
00:07:57,766 --> 00:07:59,400
-Cheers.
-Cheers.

161
00:08:03,166 --> 00:08:04,200
Could be a little hotter.

162
00:08:04,450 --> 00:08:06,185
-I was gonna say that.
-What can they do?

163
00:08:06,252 --> 00:08:08,888
-Are you gonna get into this?
-[sighs]

164
00:08:08,966 --> 00:08:11,833
It's so good.
It just needs a little more heat.

165
00:08:13,092 --> 00:08:14,160
What do you think?

166
00:08:14,393 --> 00:08:18,431
Now we'll see who the nice person is...
Oh, look at this. There she goes.

167
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:20,200
-[Julia] Can we ask a favor?
-Yes.

168
00:08:20,299 --> 00:08:22,000
Is it possible to heat these up?

169
00:08:22,099 --> 00:08:23,533
'Cause this was too cool.

170
00:08:24,833 --> 00:08:26,166
I thought I was nice.

171
00:08:26,666 --> 00:08:28,566
The guy's calling me an asshole
behind my back.

172
00:08:28,733 --> 00:08:31,166
That is the right way
to call someone an asshole.

173
00:08:31,233 --> 00:08:32,766
Behind their back.

174
00:08:32,866 --> 00:08:35,133
-You can put that in your book of...
-Asshole manners.

175
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:42,733
You remember in that cartoon
I told you that I loved?

176
00:08:42,799 --> 00:08:46,333
It's a guy painting a landscape,
and a car is going by,

177
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:49,766
and the guy is sticking his bare ass
out of the window,

178
00:08:50,029 --> 00:08:53,733
and the balloon says,
"Hey, Ottist!" O-T-T-I-S-T.

179
00:08:54,366 --> 00:08:55,500
[Julia] "Paint this!"

180
00:08:56,766 --> 00:09:01,000
The breadth of quality
in the humor that you appreciate

181
00:09:01,066 --> 00:09:04,166
is the widest of anyone
that I've ever met.

182
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:09,382
[server] Two hot cappuccinos for you.

183
00:09:09,448 --> 00:09:10,883
-[Julia] Thanks so much.
-[Jerry] Wow.

184
00:09:10,966 --> 00:09:12,833
If this isn't warm enough,

185
00:09:12,933 --> 00:09:14,966
guess who's going up to get a third one?

186
00:09:15,733 --> 00:09:16,766
I'm looking at him.

187
00:09:16,833 --> 00:09:18,966
[upbeat jazz music playing]

188
00:09:21,433 --> 00:09:22,833
Marriage just cracks me up.

189
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:25,100
-How long have you been married now?
-Fifteen years.

190
00:09:25,197 --> 00:09:27,867
Really? You had always said
you would never get married.

191
00:09:27,933 --> 00:09:29,033
I still feel that way.

192
00:09:31,433 --> 00:09:34,900
But there was quite a bit of work
that my wife, God bless her, had to do.

193
00:09:34,966 --> 00:09:36,533
That was a grimy job.

194
00:09:36,842 --> 00:09:41,247
Thigh-high boots
and a lot of long tools that grind,

195
00:09:41,333 --> 00:09:43,166
and a lot of hazmat bags.

196
00:09:43,233 --> 00:09:44,733
Just take this out of here.

197
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:46,033
This has all gotta go.

198
00:09:46,100 --> 00:09:50,100
I find that fights, for us,

199
00:09:50,356 --> 00:09:53,726
start with something absolutely minute.

200
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:55,199
Like...

201
00:09:57,199 --> 00:09:59,500
"Why are there coffee grinds here?"

202
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:02,400
I can't help myself, you understand?

203
00:10:02,466 --> 00:10:04,600
I just love the timing on the table hit.

204
00:10:04,699 --> 00:10:06,233
Not on the "here," but on the "why."

205
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:10,500
-"Why are there coffee grinds here?"
-"Why are there coffee grinds here?"

206
00:10:10,566 --> 00:10:11,900
[both chuckling]

207
00:10:11,966 --> 00:10:14,199
I got another one to tell you.
But it was a big birthday.

208
00:10:14,266 --> 00:10:16,433
He gave me this big present

209
00:10:17,166 --> 00:10:18,300
of a suitcase.

210
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:22,300
And I didn't respond to that
particularly well.

211
00:10:22,400 --> 00:10:24,433
-No.
-I don't want a suitcase.

212
00:10:24,533 --> 00:10:26,933
If I need a suitcase,
I'm just gonna buy it for myself.

213
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,366
I don't really think of this as a gift.

214
00:10:29,433 --> 00:10:31,466
-Sorry, Brad. Mm-mm.
-No.

215
00:10:31,533 --> 00:10:33,633
-And, by the way, in his defense--
-No.

216
00:10:33,699 --> 00:10:35,066
-In his defense...
-Bradley.

217
00:10:35,133 --> 00:10:38,133
No. I go out for, like, a run or something
to blow off steam.

218
00:10:38,199 --> 00:10:40,900
I come back, on the dining room table,

219
00:10:40,966 --> 00:10:44,199
the yellow book is open to florists.

220
00:10:46,266 --> 00:10:48,000
Just wide open, "Florists."

221
00:10:48,433 --> 00:10:50,833
In his defense, he's gotten crazy better.

222
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:53,800
-How many years you married?
-Twenty-eight years in June.

223
00:10:53,866 --> 00:10:55,633
So, go like that for good luck.

224
00:10:59,266 --> 00:11:01,333
[upbeat jazz playing]

225
00:11:04,966 --> 00:11:07,866
But let me just go back to the moment
you and I met,

226
00:11:07,933 --> 00:11:10,333
-at Ren-Mar.
-Ren-Mar, Desilu Studios.

227
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:12,000
Formerly Desilu Studios.

228
00:11:12,066 --> 00:11:14,699
I remember seeing you,
and you were eating cereal that day.

229
00:11:14,766 --> 00:11:16,633
You were eating cereal,
and we were talking.

230
00:11:16,699 --> 00:11:19,633
I just thought, "He's such a relaxed guy."

231
00:11:19,699 --> 00:11:21,733
And then when we went
and we read that scene,

232
00:11:21,800 --> 00:11:23,733
"Where do you wanna go?"
"I don't care, I'm not hungry."

233
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:24,933
"Long as I don't have to talk."

234
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:25,900
-Right?
-Right.

235
00:11:25,951 --> 00:11:28,621
Could go to one of those cappuccino
places. They let you sit there.

236
00:11:29,633 --> 00:11:31,366
I'll go if I don't have to talk.

237
00:11:32,266 --> 00:11:34,600
Nothing had ever been
written like that before.

238
00:11:34,666 --> 00:11:36,500
-Like, it was just a conversation...
-Right.

239
00:11:36,566 --> 00:11:39,300
...as opposed to a setup,
punchline, setup, punchline.

240
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:41,833
-Right.
-And I just remember thinking,

241
00:11:42,166 --> 00:11:45,000
"This is so nice and relaxed.

242
00:11:45,066 --> 00:11:47,333
It's got such a different vibe.
It'll never go."

243
00:11:47,733 --> 00:11:49,400
-Well, it almost didn't.
-I know.

244
00:11:49,466 --> 00:11:52,000
[upbeat jazz playing]

245
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:54,933
So, what do these do?

246
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:56,733
They're progressives.

247
00:11:57,133 --> 00:11:58,333
Wow, you look great.

248
00:11:58,400 --> 00:11:59,933
[both laughing]

249
00:12:00,066 --> 00:12:01,633
Look down to the bottom of the lens.

250
00:12:02,933 --> 00:12:03,933
[Julia laughing]

251
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:05,766
There's a vat of coffee.

252
00:12:05,833 --> 00:12:08,600
This is the weakest prescription
of glasses

253
00:12:08,661 --> 00:12:10,696
-I have ever seen in my life.
-You're wrong.

254
00:12:11,733 --> 00:12:12,733
I'm fine with that, too.

255
00:12:13,199 --> 00:12:14,699
There's marriage, A to Z.

256
00:12:14,934 --> 00:12:16,469
"You're wrong." "I'm fine."

257
00:12:17,066 --> 00:12:18,866
[both laughing]

258
00:12:19,633 --> 00:12:21,233
If I'm right, I know I'm right.

259
00:12:21,300 --> 00:12:24,933
And I know the right place
for coffee grounds.

260
00:12:25,033 --> 00:12:26,133
[Julia laughing]

261
00:12:26,233 --> 00:12:27,133
Not there.

262
00:12:30,166 --> 00:12:31,466
Let me get this.

263
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:34,199
Thank you.

264
00:12:36,566 --> 00:12:40,300
[uptempo instrumental music playing]

265
00:12:40,366 --> 00:12:44,133
[inaudible]

266
00:12:44,866 --> 00:12:46,333
Oh, look at that pretty dog. Hi.

267
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:48,266
-[Julia] What's your doggy's name?
-[woman] Candy.

268
00:12:48,366 --> 00:12:49,633
[Julia] She's a therapy dog?

269
00:12:49,699 --> 00:12:51,433
Does Candy have time for Jerry right now?

270
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:53,800
-[woman] Yes, it's five minutes.
-Can you squeeze me in?

271
00:12:54,266 --> 00:12:55,533
[Julia] Bye, Candy. See you.

272
00:13:00,966 --> 00:13:02,900
Every woman today lives like Cleopatra.

273
00:13:02,966 --> 00:13:03,800
You got that right.

274
00:13:03,866 --> 00:13:07,300
The emollients,
and salves, and balms, and oils.

275
00:13:07,366 --> 00:13:09,566
Just swimming in goo.

276
00:13:15,033 --> 00:13:17,166
-[Julia] You hungry?
-[Jerry] I could always eat.

277
00:13:17,530 --> 00:13:19,665
-Wanna go get something to eat?
-Where do you wanna go?

278
00:13:19,732 --> 00:13:20,833
Don't care, I'm not hungry.

279
00:13:20,933 --> 00:13:22,833
-[Jerry] Do you wanna eat?
-I don't know.

280
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:25,000
I mean, I'll eat--
If you wanna eat, I'll eat with you.

281
00:13:25,066 --> 00:13:27,566
[jazz music continues]

282
00:13:29,099 --> 00:13:30,566
I know how much you love your bed.

283
00:13:30,633 --> 00:13:33,366
Is that still your very favorite place
to be, in bed?

284
00:13:34,566 --> 00:13:35,933
I can't believe you're saying that.

285
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,966
What do you mean?
You think I don't know you?

286
00:13:38,033 --> 00:13:40,366
-We chatted a bit over ten years.
-Seventy-five years.

287
00:13:40,433 --> 00:13:43,199
You said the only way
you would ever do another TV series

288
00:13:43,266 --> 00:13:45,033
is if you could do it from your bed.

289
00:13:45,099 --> 00:13:46,033
[Jerry laughs]

290
00:13:46,133 --> 00:13:47,633
-That's what you said.
-Uh-huh.

291
00:13:47,699 --> 00:13:49,699
-What's your favorite place?
-Santa Barbara.

292
00:13:49,800 --> 00:13:51,833
-Favorite cookie?
-Chocolate-chip.

293
00:13:51,900 --> 00:13:53,000
You're a thrill a minute.

294
00:13:53,733 --> 00:13:55,266
[laughing]

295
00:13:55,866 --> 00:13:59,066
-What's your favorite brand of deodorant?
-Mitchum.

296
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:00,866
It's very bad for you, deodorant.

297
00:14:01,106 --> 00:14:03,442
I actually try not to wear it
unless I'm working.

298
00:14:03,533 --> 00:14:04,533
I know.

299
00:14:04,599 --> 00:14:06,066
[laughing]

300
00:14:06,166 --> 00:14:07,199
I'm working now.

301
00:14:11,199 --> 00:14:13,066
But don't you like to shop
on the Internet?

302
00:14:13,133 --> 00:14:14,766
Do you go on NET-A-PORTER like my wife?

303
00:14:15,833 --> 00:14:17,199
You love it, right?

304
00:14:17,266 --> 00:14:19,500
All you good-taste girls love that.

305
00:14:19,566 --> 00:14:20,866
It's good.

306
00:14:20,966 --> 00:14:22,533
Except ask her this question for me.

307
00:14:22,599 --> 00:14:24,666
They send me a thing,
I click on it right away,

308
00:14:24,997 --> 00:14:27,600
-and it already says sold out.
-When you get a thing from them...

309
00:14:27,666 --> 00:14:29,266
-And you click on it.
-...and you click on it...

310
00:14:29,333 --> 00:14:32,866
And does she go to it, and it immediately
says X, Y, and Z are sold out?

311
00:14:32,966 --> 00:14:34,400
And then it'll say sold out?

312
00:14:38,966 --> 00:14:40,500
Do you get the VIP preview?

313
00:14:40,566 --> 00:14:41,866
Ah! See?

314
00:14:41,933 --> 00:14:43,033
Can I talk to her?

315
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:45,666
Hi, Jessica. It's Julia.

316
00:14:45,733 --> 00:14:47,533
What VIP preview?

317
00:14:47,633 --> 00:14:49,066
[laughs heartily]

318
00:14:49,566 --> 00:14:51,566
I'm just clicking onto them right now.

319
00:14:52,566 --> 00:14:54,666
[sighs, softly] I'm gonna
need some more coffee.

320
00:14:55,933 --> 00:14:57,466
Who is buying this crap?

321
00:14:57,733 --> 00:14:59,266
-No offense.
-And it's...

322
00:14:59,333 --> 00:15:02,500
I'm getting a thing from Jerry
saying wrap it up.

323
00:15:02,933 --> 00:15:04,133
Oh, yeah, and by the way,

324
00:15:04,266 --> 00:15:08,199
thank you for training him
to become a human being.

325
00:15:09,642 --> 00:15:12,811
'Cause what I was dealing with was, um...

326
00:15:13,979 --> 00:15:16,715
Well, anyway. Okay, take care.

327
00:15:16,949 --> 00:15:19,084
You're really making me miss the show now.

328
00:15:19,166 --> 00:15:20,000
I know, right?

329
00:15:20,066 --> 00:15:21,933
The laughs we used to have hanging out.

330
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:23,533
-The laughs.
-Yeah.

331
00:15:23,599 --> 00:15:27,699
It is so fun to be reminded
of how funny you are.

332
00:15:27,833 --> 00:15:29,566
Oh, likewise.

333
00:15:30,466 --> 00:15:32,566
[upbeat music playing]

334
00:15:34,199 --> 00:15:35,868
What about when we were
at that restaurant?

335
00:15:35,966 --> 00:15:37,733
-Ah!
-Will you ever forget it?

336
00:15:37,800 --> 00:15:38,766
-Never.
-Never.

337
00:15:38,833 --> 00:15:42,400
We were in New York because
we did the Rolling Stone cover shoot.

338
00:15:43,966 --> 00:15:47,766
And then we thought,
"We'll go out, and we'll all eat together,

339
00:15:48,233 --> 00:15:52,866
and we'll sit out on the sidewalk,
and people will freak out!"

340
00:15:52,966 --> 00:15:56,900
-And not a soul, except for one.
-Well. One.

341
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,500
-One homeless man.
-One homeless man.

342
00:15:59,566 --> 00:16:01,933
-God bless him.
-Yeah, he was a big fan.

343
00:16:02,033 --> 00:16:03,699
[smooth instrumental music playing]

344
00:16:10,035 --> 00:16:11,737
[Julia] Here's something
my mom said to me,

345
00:16:11,833 --> 00:16:13,800
and I think it's very true,
in terms of happiness.

346
00:16:13,866 --> 00:16:16,633
You have to always have
something to look forward to.

347
00:16:16,709 --> 00:16:19,478
-Hmm.
-And it can be a very minor thing,

348
00:16:19,566 --> 00:16:21,066
and it can be a major thing,

349
00:16:21,133 --> 00:16:24,699
but you always have to have
something you're looking forward to next.

350
00:16:24,883 --> 00:16:27,086
Well, what are you looking forward to
right now?

351
00:16:27,166 --> 00:16:28,833
Getting the hell out of this car.

352
00:16:29,266 --> 00:16:30,666
[both laughing]

353
00:16:31,699 --> 00:16:34,000
-Oh, that was a heavy peach.
-[Julia laughs]

354
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:36,633
That was a heavy peach, and she picked it.

355
00:16:36,699 --> 00:16:38,066
[giggles]

356
00:16:38,897 --> 00:16:40,499
[Julia] What are you looking forward to?

357
00:16:40,599 --> 00:16:41,933
[Jerry] Getting out of this car.

358
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:43,033
[both laughing]

359
00:16:51,043 --> 00:16:52,644
Thanks for inviting me to be on your show.

360
00:16:52,733 --> 00:16:54,400
-I think this show is great.
-Thank you.

361
00:16:54,466 --> 00:16:56,033
-And I love ya.
-I love you, too.

362
00:16:56,099 --> 00:16:57,366
I really do.

